1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The whole world saw — one tiny
cluster.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Follow Orion’s Belt past red Aldebaran
to a tiny knot of stars.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
The West calls it the Pleiades: the
Seven Sisters.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Most people count only six; the seventh
is hard to see from cities.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
From New Zealand to Hawaiʻi, its name is
almost the same word.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Linguists trace it to one old word,
mata-liki: “little eyes.” Māori lore
says a god’s eyes.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Romania sees a hen with her chicks.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Siberia: a duck’s nest. Brazil’s Tupi: a
wasp nest.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Japan: Subaru, from a word for gathering
into one bundle.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
China’s Sima Qian: Mao, “hairy head.”
Babylon: zappu, a mane. Indonesia’s
Bugis: a tuft.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Each June it returns before dawn:
Matariki, New Zealand’s Māori New Year.

12
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
This month it’s up all night. By
midnight it rides high in the south.

13
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The White Tiger: Orion in a Chinese sky.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

